from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('7.1')
for l,rows in {
'01':['A retail price index measures changes in the cost of a representative consumer basket relative to a base period.','A simple fixed-basket index is current basket cost divided by base basket cost, multiplied by 100.','An index change must be compared with the earlier index to find the percentage price increase; index points are not automatically percentage changes.'],
'02':['Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level.','Demand-pull inflation arises when total demand presses against the economy’s ability to supply output; cost-push inflation begins with increased production costs.','Disinflation is slower inflation, while deflation is a sustained fall in the general price level.'],
'03':['Inflation reduces purchasing power when nominal income does not keep pace with the relevant prices.','Unexpected inflation can reduce the real value of a fixed nominal debt, but repayment affordability also depends on income and contract terms.','Inflation can create uncertainty and redistribute real resources between groups, with effects depending on anticipation, adjustment and relative price changes.']}.items():
 for row in rows:t.claim(l,row)
t.block('01','plain_explanation','Measure a basket against its base',"""
A retail price index summarises changes in the cost of a representative consumer basket compared with a base period. A basket contains specified quantities of goods and services. Holding quantities fixed in a simple example separates price changes from changes in what is purchased. The base index is set to 100; it is a reference number rather than a money amount.

Use an invented Namibian household basket costing N$500 in the base year, N$550 in Year 1 and N$577.50 in Year 2. The simple index is current basket cost / base basket cost × 100. Year 1 gives 550 / 500 × 100 = 110; Year 2 gives 577.50 / 500 × 100 = 115.5. An index of 115.5 means the same basket costs 15.5% more than in the base year.

The annual change from Year 1 is not 15.5%. The index rises 5.5 points from 110 to 115.5. Dividing 5.5 by the earlier 110 gives 0.05, equivalent to 5%. This distinguishes a change in index points from a percentage change. Similarly, the base-to-Year-1 rise is 10%, so the annual inflation rate slows from 10% to 5% even though the basket keeps becoming more expensive.

A representative basket is an average. A household buying more of the fastest-rising items can experience a larger increase in its own cost of living. Basket selection, quality changes and changing consumption patterns can affect the usefulness of the measure. In a simple calculation use the supplied comparable basket rather than introducing unprovided quantities or treating the index as a precise experience shared by every household.

Basket costs can be built from individual prices and quantities. An invented basket containing ten units of item A and five of item B costs N$200 if A is N$10 and B N$20. If A rises to N$12 while B stays N$20, the same quantities cost N$220, giving an index of 110. Simply averaging the two percentage price changes would give a different result because their contributions to expenditure need not be equal. Cost the specified quantities first.
""")
t.block('02','comparison','Distinguish the price trend from its cause',"""
Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level. A single product becoming dearer does not establish that general pattern; other prices could be unchanged or falling. Demand-pull inflation begins with demand growing beyond what firms can readily supply. Buyers compete for limited available output, putting upward pressure on prices. The capacity condition matters: stronger demand can instead raise real output where resources are idle and supply responds.

Cost-push inflation begins with increased production costs, such as a broad rise in imported input prices. If firms pass those costs into selling prices, the general level can rise even without stronger consumer demand. The cost increase must spread sufficiently to support a general-price explanation; one workshop’s isolated bill is not the whole economy. The two causes can operate together, so identify the initiating change and the evidence rather than assuming a single cause.

Slower positive inflation is disinflation, not deflation. In the invented basket, prices still rise in Year 2 despite the slower rate. Deflation would mean a sustained decline in the general price level. These distinctions affect a household’s interpretation: a lower inflation announcement does not promise restoration of the previous year’s prices. Topic 6.2 considers policy instruments; this topic owns the measurement, causes and consequences.
""")
t.block('03','evaluation','Consequences depend on incomes, contracts and expectations',"""
If an invented pension stays N$3,000 while the pensioner’s basket prices rise 10%, the same money buys less. To buy the old bundle would require N$3,300. The purchasing-power loss follows from income failing to adjust, not from the pension’s money amount falling. A household whose income rises with relevant prices may avoid that particular loss, although its personal basket and timing still matter.

Inflation can affect savers and lenders where returns fail to compensate for higher prices. A fixed money balance can buy less later. A debtor with a fixed nominal sum can owe less in real purchasing-power terms after unexpected inflation. Yet the nominal debt remains due, and the debtor’s repayment burden relative to income improves only if income rises or other relevant conditions change. A person with fixed income and rising essentials can find repayment harder despite lower real debt value.

Uncertain inflation can complicate budgets, pricing and investment decisions. Businesses may struggle to distinguish a general price rise from demand for their own product. Competitiveness can weaken if domestic costs and export prices rise faster than competitors’ without offsetting productivity or exchange-rate changes. These are conditional effects, not proof that each firm’s profit falls.

Anticipated low stable inflation can be easier to plan for than an unexpected rapid rise. Contracts may adjust, and some workers may negotiate higher pay. The judgement should compare the affected groups and their ability to adapt. Fixed-income households face a strong direct purchasing-power risk; debtors can gain in real terms under a fixed nominal contract, but income, prices and financing conditions decide practical affordability. No group label alone settles the outcome.

Relative price changes can alter the effect further. A business selling a product whose price is rising may still face input costs increasing faster, squeezing its margin. Another can pass costs on more readily. Inflation is a general measure, so an individual profit conclusion needs both receipts and costs rather than the general rate alone.
""")
def s(n):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['slot'].split('-')[-2]==f'{n:03}')
def c(n,l,p,a,g):t.card(s(n),l,p,a,g)
c(1,'01','Define a retail price index.','A measure of changes in the cost of a representative consumer basket relative to a base period.',['Definition/key term (1): representative basket price change against a base.'])
c(2,'01','Calculate the Year 1 price index with base 100 from an invented identical basket costing N$500 in the base year and N$550 in Year 1.','Index = current basket cost / base cost × 100 = 550 / 500 × 100 = 110.',['Formula/substitution (1): 550/500×100. Result/units interpretation (1): index 110, not N$110.'])
c(3,'01','Analyse the annual price change where an invented index rises from 110 to 115.5, base 100 unchanged.','It rises 5.5 index points. The proportionate increase is 5.5/110 = 0.05, equivalent to 5%, rather than 5.5% or 15.5% over this year.',['Pattern (1): index increases. Evidence (1): 5.5 points. Explanation (2): use earlier 110 denominator (1), gives 5% annual rise (1).'])
c(4,'02','Define inflation.','A sustained rise in the general price level.',['Definition/key term (1): sustained general price rise.'])
c(5,'02','Explain the distinction between demand-pull and cost-push inflation by their initiating causes.','Demand-pull begins with spending pressure beyond responsive capacity; cost-push begins with higher production costs passed into general prices.',['Difference (1): demand relative to capacity. Contrast/test (1): production-cost pressure.'])
c(6,'02','Explain the supported inflation cause where imported input costs rise broadly and firms raise selling prices despite unchanged customer demand.','This is cost-push pressure: higher production costs initiate the general-price increase, rather than stronger demand.',['Context (1): broad input cost increase. Reason/conclusion (1): costs rather than demand initiate price pressure.'])
c(7,'03','Explain the effect of 10% relevant price inflation on an invented fixed N$3,000 monthly pension, with no other income adjustment.','The previous bundle would cost N$3,300. The pension remains N$3,000, so purchasing power falls and the recipient must reduce quantities or other spending to accommodate higher prices.',['Starting change (1): relevant prices rise. Because/mechanism (2): old bundle now N$3,300 (1), money income fixed (1). Consequence (1): less purchasing power.'])
c(8,'03','Explain why unexpected inflation does not automatically improve loan affordability for a debtor whose money income and fixed nominal repayment stay unchanged while essentials become dearer.','The debt’s real value may fall, but its cash repayment does not. Higher essential costs leave less income for repayment, so practical affordability can worsen.',['Context (1): fixed income/repayment and dearer essentials. Reason/conclusion (1): real debt reduction differs from budget affordability.'])
base='Invented identical basket costs: base year N$500, Year 1 N$550, Year 2 N$577.50. A general rise in imported input costs occurs with customer demand unchanged. Household A receives a fixed N$3,000 monthly pension. Household B has fixed nominal debt and repayment; its money income rises 10% while its relevant prices rise 5%. No contract repricing or other changes are assumed.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][0],'01 02 03','Define retail price index (2). Identify the causes in these invented cases: spending rises beyond available output; broad input costs rise and are passed on (2). Explain an effect of 10% basket inflation on a fixed money pension (2). Identify the base and current basket costs from this invented comparison: base N$500, current N$550 (2).','The index compares a representative basket’s cost with a base. The cases show demand-pull and cost-push inflation. A fixed pension buys less after prices rise, requiring an adjustment in consumption. The basket costs are N$500 base and N$550 current.',['Meaning (2): representative basket price measure (1), base comparison (1). Separate points for causes (2): demand-pull (1), cost-push (1). Explanation (2): fixed money income (1), lower purchasing power (1). Identification (2): base N$500 (1), current N$550 (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01 02 03',base+' Describe three features: base index, Year 2 index and the initiating inflation cause (6). Explain the annual inflation calculation and the cost-push mechanism (7). Discuss whether the two households are affected equally (7).','Base index is 100, Year 2 index 115.5 and cost-push begins with broad imported input costs. Annual inflation in Year 2 is 5.5/110, equivalent to 5%; price levels still rise. Input costs passed into prices explain cost-push with unchanged demand. A loses purchasing power; B’s fixed repayment takes a smaller income share while income outpaces prices. Effects differ, conditional on the stated fixed contract and relevant baskets.',['Data description (6): base reference (1), 100 detail (1); Year 2 index (1), 115.5 detail (1); cost-push (1), input-cost detail (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): Year 1 index 110 (1), change 5.5 points (1), earlier 110 denominator (1), 5% annual rate (1); input cost rises (1), firms pass through (1), general prices rise without demand growth (1). Both sides/judgement (7): A fixed income (1), purchasing power falls (1); B income rises faster than prices (1), fixed repayment smaller income share (1); condition contract unchanged/personal baskets (1); judgement unequal effects (1), tied to income adjustment and debt terms (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
cases='Invented 10% relevant price inflation. Case 1: pension income stays N$3,000 monthly, no other resources change. Case 2: a debtor’s fixed nominal balance and fixed cash repayment do not change; money income rises 10% and no interest repricing or other costs occur. Both have the same relevant price rise.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][2],'03',cases+' Evaluate the inflation effect on the pensioner (7). Evaluate the claim that the debtor benefits (7), distinguishing nominal debt, real debt and affordability.','Case 1: pension money is unchanged but the old bundle costs N$3,300, so real consumption capacity falls. The impact could be smaller with a different basket or compensating resources, but neither is supplied. Case 2: nominal debt is unchanged, its real purchasing-power value falls, and repayment takes a smaller share of rising income. A benefit is supported under these fixed-contract conditions, although other debtors with fixed incomes or repriced rates might not gain.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for adverse effect fixed income (1), old bundle N$3,300 (1); limiting argument personal basket may differ (1), compensation could change outcome (1); condition none supplied (1); judgement purchasing-power loss (1), tied to fixed income (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for lower real debt (1), lower repayment/income share (1); argument against nominal sum still due (1), other contract/income cases differ (1); condition income rise and fixed contract (1); judgement benefit supported here (1), separate real value from nominal and affordability (1). Total 14.'],[cases])
assert round(550/500*100,6)==110 and round(577.5/500*100,6)==115.5
